{"kind": "report", "step": 18, "ts": "2026-11-18T03:52:48.547267+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "gw.lan", "component": "service_config", "cause": "The unbound resolver on gw.lan applies DNS-rebinding protection (private-address: 10.0.0.0/8) without a matching private-domain exception for lan., so forwarded .lan answers pointing at private IPs are filtered down to empty NOERROR responses; when dns1's aggressive rate limit (20 q/s per /24) intermittently drops queries during traffic bursts, clients fall back to gw.lan and get an empty answer, which surfaces as getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND until the next successful lookup via dns1.", "evidence": ["dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan returns 'nas.lan. 10 IN A 10.20.0.50' (NOERROR with answer) \u2014 dns1 resolves the name correctly", "dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan returns status NOERROR with ANSWER: 0 \u2014 gw.lan replies that the name has no address", "drill nas.lan on gw.lan itself (server 127.0.0.1) also shows QUERY: 1, ANSWER: 0 \u2014 the emptying happens in gw's own resolver, not in transit", "cat /var/unbound/unbound.conf on gw.lan shows 'private-address: 10.0.0.0/8' and 'private-address: 127.0.0.0/8' with a comment 'DNS rebinding protection: drop answers for public names that point at private addresses', plus forward-zone lan. -> 10.20.0.3; grep found no private-domain line to exempt lan.", "journalctl -u primary-dns on dns1.lan shows repeated bursts: 'ratelimit: 10.20.0.0/24 exceeded 20 q/s, dropping 23/31/8 queries' at 02:14, 09:41 and 13:02 \u2014 brief windows matching the intermittent alerts", "cat /etc/resolv.conf on mon.lan shows clients use nameserver 10.20.0.3 then 10.20.0.1, so a dropped/delayed dns1 reply makes glibc retry against gw.lan and accept its empty answer as authoritative"], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "On gw.lan, stop the rebinding filter from eating local answers: add `private-domain: \"lan.\"` (and any other local zones) to /var/unbound/unbound.conf, or scope/remove the `private-address: 10.0.0.0/8` rule so forwarded .lan answers pass through untouched. Secondarily, raise or remove the 20 q/s ratelimit in /opt/primary-dns/config.yaml on dns1.lan (it applies per /24 to an entire LAN of clients) so normal bursts don't push clients onto the fallback server. Internet lookups are unaffected either way, so no provider action is needed.", "detail": "unbound private-address filtering empties local .lan answers", "contributing": {"host": "dns1.lan", "component": "dns_rate_limit"}}}
